      "final_answer": "Reviewed **1992082** (\"Add average\") in `Code/github.com/acme/widget`, added to the `Code/README.md` index.\n\n**The bug: `average()` panics on an empty slice.**\n\n```rust\npub fn average(xs: &[i64]) -> i64 {\n    let total: i64 = xs.iter().sum();\n    total / xs.len() as i64   // xs.len() == 0 -> divide by zero -> panic\n}\n```\n\n`average(&[])` divides an i64 by zero, which Rust turns into a runtime panic (debug and release). The caller has no way to avoid it; the function should return `Option<i64>` or `Option<f64>`, or document/require non-empty input.\n\nTwo smaller points on the same function:\n\n- **It truncates, so it is not the \"arithmetic mean\" its doc claims.** `average(&[1, 2])` returns `1`, and negatives round toward zero (`&[-1, -2, -2]` gives `-1`, not `-1.67`). Returning `f64` (like `sum` in this module already does) would match the doc, and the `&[i64]` vs `sum`'s `&[f64]` mismatch would go away too.\n- **Overflow:** summing large i64 values wraps (release) or panics (debug). Acceptable sometimes, worth a doc note at least.\n\nCommit hash again: `199208282f76bdb52c3b7c9d35026604b131f443`.\n\n[qwen-local · write · $0.0000]",
